Fixed and committed as dcf4f8cb. When a Bedrock model id has no preset, yoyo's warning said replies are "capped at 16000 output tokens", and that was false: yoyo never sends that cap.

What I saw in the actual requests (new tests/bedrock_max_tokens_request.rs): yoyo talks to a local stub that records each request, using fake AWS credentials. No real AWS endpoint is contacted. The stub returns a 400 error, so yoyo sends exactly one request and doesn't retry.
• No `--max-tokens`: the request has no inferenceConfig at all, so the guessed 16000 is never sent. Bedrock uses the model's own default output limit.
• `--max-tokens 1234`: inferenceConfig.maxTokens: 1234 is sent. This shows the stub really reads the field, so the absence in the first case is real.

The fix: in src/model_limits_note.rs, Bedrock now uses the same wording Google got in #1158. The warning still names the guessed 200,000-token context window, since yoyo does use that (for compaction). It then says yoyo sends no output cap, so Bedrock applies the model's own default.

Tests and docs:
• A new unit test pins the full Bedrock warning text: once with the guessed window, once with a user-set window, and once with a user cap, which turns the warning off.
• An older test in src/guessed_limits_arms_tests.rs checked for the false "capped at" sentence. It now checks the new wording.
• The "200,000 / 16000 guess" sentence in docs/src/configuration/models.md is corrected to match.

Checks:
• I deliberately broke the Bedrock branch and the new test failed on the "capped at" line, as it should. I restored it in the same command; the sabotage marker count is back to 0.
• fmt, clippy with -D warnings, build and the full cargo test all pass.

One open question, not filed: when thinking is on, the Bedrock request sends a thinking token budget without any maxTokens. I haven't checked whether Bedrock accepts that. I couldn't open an issue for it because gh returned 401 (bad credentials) around 21:5x. It's written up in the commit body for a later phase to file.
